;;; A macro's call under a written `(not …)` is stored as the negation of a fact nothing holds, so
;;; the state it contradicts is alive —
;;; M1f **W86**, register row `W86/macro-call-negated-fact`, filed by S1f.7.12 (T1f.7.12.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, of whom Bob has paid, a macro `settled`, and the fact `(not (settled Bob))`. No
;;; candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The author means that Bob has not paid, which contradicts `(paid Bob)`: written `(not (paid
;;; Bob))` the program has an empty R, its root dead. The fact is not expanded, so it is refused
;;; at load, at the call, one line, on every command.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `91b12be8`, release: the program loads; `(not (settled Bob))` is stored beside `(paid
;;; Bob)` and contradicts nothing. R is {∅}, root alive, exit 0 on every command.
;;; Refused at load since S1f.7.12 (T1f.7.12.3).

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(macro settled (?g) (paid ?g) :why "{?g} has settled: they have paid")
(paid Bob)
(not (settled Bob))
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (paid ?g))
